CAT = "auwhiteLaw"; SEG = "LAW"
Q = [
("Under the model WHS laws, what does PCBU stand for?",
 "Person conducting a business or undertaking", ["Principal contractor for building use", "Person controlling a building or unit", "Person contracting for a building undertaking"],
 "A PCBU is a person conducting a business or undertaking: a company, sole trader, partnership or government body that runs the work. The term replaced 'employer' as the main duty holder in the model WHS Act."),
("Who has the primary duty of care for the health and safety of workers on a construction site?",
 "The PCBU", ["The safety regulator", "The workers' union", "The site's HSR"],
 "The model WHS Act puts the primary duty of care on the PCBU, so far as is reasonably practicable. The regulator enforces the law and an HSR represents workers, but neither carries the primary duty."),
("Which of these is a duty every worker has under the WHS laws?",
 "Take reasonable care for others", ["Supply their own first aid kit", "Prepare the site safety plan", "Inspect all plant every month"],
 "Workers must take reasonable care for their own health and safety and that their acts or omissions do not harm others. Safety plans, first aid and plant inspection systems are the PCBU's job."),
("Your supervisor gives you a reasonable safety instruction. What must you do?",
 "Follow it as far as you are able", ["Follow it only if it is written", "Check it first with the union", "Follow it only on paid overtime"],
 "Workers must comply, so far as they are reasonably able, with any reasonable instruction given to meet the WHS laws. It does not have to be in writing to apply."),
("When working out what is 'reasonably practicable', which of these is weighed?",
 "How likely the harm is to occur", ["The worker's length of service", "The company's annual profit", "How far behind schedule it is"],
 "The test weighs the likelihood of the hazard or risk, the degree of harm, what is known about it, what controls exist and are suitable, and then cost. Profit and deadlines are not part of it."),
("What duty does an officer, such as a company director, have under the WHS Act?",
 "Exercise due diligence", ["Hold a high risk licence", "Elect the site's HSR", "Inspect each site daily"],
 "Officers must exercise due diligence to make sure the PCBU complies with its duties, for example by knowing its hazards and making sure resources and processes are in place. Workers, not officers, elect HSRs."),
("Who at a workplace must take reasonable care for their own health and safety?",
 "Everyone, visitors included", ["Only the paid employees on site", "Only licensed trades", "Only site supervisors"],
 "The WHS Act gives workers and other persons at a workplace, such as visitors and delivery drivers, a duty to take reasonable care for their own safety and not harm others."),
("When must a PCBU consult workers about health and safety?",
 "When identifying hazards and controls", ["Only after a serious injury has occurred", "Only when the regulator demands it", "Only once a year at a staff meeting"],
 "Consultation is required when identifying hazards, assessing risks, deciding on controls, proposing changes that affect safety and in other set situations. It is ongoing, not a once-a-year event."),
("Who elects a health and safety representative (HSR)?",
 "Workers in the work group", ["The site manager", "The safety regulator", "The principal contractor's manager"],
 "An HSR is elected by and from the workers in their work group. Management cannot appoint the HSR."),
("What is the main role of a health and safety representative (HSR)?",
 "Represent workers on safety", ["Approve every SWMS on site", "Issue high risk work licences", "Investigate deaths for police"],
 "An HSR represents the workers in their work group on health and safety matters, can inspect the workplace and raise issues with the PCBU. Licences come from the regulator, and SWMS are the PCBU's responsibility."),
("Once trained, what may a health and safety representative do?",
 "Issue a provisional improvement notice", ["Issue an on-the-spot fine to the employer", "Cancel a worker's construction card", "Grant a high risk work licence"],
 "An HSR who has completed approved training can issue a provisional improvement notice (PIN) and direct unsafe work to stop. Fines, card cancellation and licences belong to the regulator."),
("A PCBU must set up a health and safety committee when asked by:",
 "An HSR or 5 or more workers", ["Any single worker on the site", "The local council", "The client's architect"],
 "Under the model WHS Act, a PCBU must establish a health and safety committee within 2 months of a request from an HSR or from 5 or more workers."),
("When may a worker stop work on safety grounds under the WHS Act?",
 "If there is a serious, imminent risk", ["Whenever a task is uncomfortable", "Only once a supervisor has agreed to it", "Only after an inspector arrives"],
 "A worker may cease work if they reasonably believe it would expose them to a serious risk from an immediate or imminent hazard. They do not need permission first."),
("After stopping work because of a serious safety risk, what must the worker do?",
 "Tell the PCBU as soon as possible", ["Leave site until it is fixed", "Fix the hazard themselves first", "Call the media about the hazard"],
 "A worker who ceases unsafe work must notify the PCBU as soon as practicable and stay available for suitable alternative work. Fixing it yourself can create new risks."),
("Can a PCBU dismiss a worker for raising a genuine safety concern?",
 "No, that is unlawful", ["Yes, if it is in writing", "Yes, during probation", "Only after one warning"],
 "The WHS Act prohibits discriminatory conduct against a worker for raising a WHS issue or concern. Probation or a warning does not make it lawful."),
("An inspector sees an activity with a serious risk to health or safety. Which notice stops it?",
 "Prohibition notice", ["Improvement notice", "Non-disturbance notice", "Infringement notice"],
 "A prohibition notice stops an activity that involves, or will involve, a serious risk until it is fixed. An improvement notice requires a breach to be remedied by a set date."),
("An inspector finds a breach that is not an immediate danger. Which notice requires it to be fixed by a set date?",
 "Improvement notice", ["Prohibition notice", "Non-disturbance notice", "Infringement notice"],
 "An improvement notice tells the duty holder to remedy a contravention within a stated time. A prohibition notice is for serious risks that must stop now."),
("Which notice tells a PCBU to leave an incident site untouched for a set time?",
 "Non-disturbance notice", ["Improvement notice", "Prohibition notice", "Provisional improvement notice"],
 "A non-disturbance notice requires the site to be preserved so an inspector can investigate. It can stop plant, substances or the area being moved or interfered with."),
("Who must complete general construction induction training (a White Card)?",
 "Anyone doing construction work", ["Only licensed tradespeople on site", "Only workers over 18", "Only supervisors"],
 "General construction induction training is required before any person carries out construction work, including labourers, apprentices and site managers. It is not limited by trade or role."),
("In most states, what applies if you have not done construction work for 2 years?",
 "You must redo the induction training", ["Your card simply lasts for life", "You only need a new site-specific induction", "You just pay a renewal fee"],
 "Most regulators treat a card as no longer valid if the holder has not carried out construction work for 2 years, so the course must be done again. Exact wording varies by state."),
("A White Card issued in one Australian state or territory is:",
 "Recognised in all of them", ["Valid in that state only", "Valid for 5 years only", "Converted after 3 months"],
 "General construction induction cards are recognised in every state and territory, so you do not need a new card when you move. You still need a site induction on each new site."),
("Your White Card covers general training. What else must you get before starting on a new site?",
 "A site-specific induction", ["A high risk work licence", "A first aid certificate", "A national police check"],
 "The unit expects site-specific induction training before work starts on each site, covering that site's hazards, rules, amenities and emergency procedures. A licence is needed only for high risk work."),
("Which state still uses its own Occupational Health and Safety Act instead of the model WHS Act?",
 "Victoria", ["New South Wales", "Queensland", "Tasmania"],
 "Victoria uses the Occupational Health and Safety Act 2004, which places similar duties on employers. Western Australia moved to a WHS Act based on the model law in March 2022."),
("Can a PCBU hand its WHS duty over to a subcontractor?",
 "No, duties can't be transferred", ["Yes, through a written contract", "Yes, if the subcontractor agrees", "Yes, for jobs shorter than a week"],
 "WHS duties cannot be transferred to another person. Several parties can hold the same duty at once, and each must meet it."),
("On a site with several contractors, how do their WHS duties work?",
 "They share duties and must consult", ["Only the head contractor has duties", "The client holds all of the duties", "Each covers its own workers only"],
 "Where more than one duty holder has a duty about the same matter, each must consult, cooperate and coordinate with the others, so far as is reasonably practicable."),
("Which of these is workplace bullying?",
 "Repeated unreasonable behaviour", ["A single reasonable direction", "Fair feedback on poor work", "Being moved to another task"],
 "Bullying is repeated, unreasonable behaviour towards a worker or group that creates a risk to health and safety. Reasonable management action, such as fair feedback, is not bullying."),
("Which WHS offence category covers reckless conduct that exposes someone to a risk of death or serious injury?",
 "Category 1", ["Category 2", "Category 3", "Category 4"],
 "Category 1 is the most serious offence under the model WHS Act, for reckless conduct, and carries the highest penalties including prison. Category 2 and 3 cover failures to meet a duty without recklessness."),
("What does a health and safety committee do?",
 "Helps develop safety procedures", ["Issues improvement notices to the PCBU", "Elects the site supervisor", "Grants high risk licences"],
 "A health and safety committee helps the PCBU and workers cooperate on safety and helps develop standards, rules and procedures. Notices and licences come from the regulator."),
("Under the WHS Act, who counts as a 'worker'?",
 "Includes contractors and volunteers", ["Only permanent full-time staff", "Only people with a White Card", "Only staff on the company payroll"],
 "A worker is anyone who carries out work for a PCBU: employees, contractors, subcontractors, labour hire workers, apprentices, work experience students and volunteers."),
("A PCBU plans a change that affects how work is done safely. What should it do first?",
 "Consult the affected workers", ["Tell workers after it starts", "Ask the regulator to approve it", "Get the union to sign it off"],
 "Proposed changes that may affect health and safety are a set trigger for consultation. Workers must get a chance to express views before the decision is made."),
("Who enforces the WHS laws on construction sites?",
 "The state or territory regulator", ["Safe Work Australia", "The local council", "The Australian Building Codes Board"],
 "Each state and territory regulator, such as SafeWork NSW or WorkSafe Queensland, inspects sites and enforces the law. Safe Work Australia develops national policy but does not enforce it."),
("What does Safe Work Australia do?",
 "Develops national WHS policy", ["Issues White Cards to workers", "Inspects building sites", "Prosecutes unsafe employers"],
 "Safe Work Australia is the national body that develops the model WHS laws, codes of practice and policy. Cards, inspections and prosecutions are handled by each state and territory regulator."),
]
